LARAVEL: DIFFERENT WAYS TO SEND DATA TO A VIEW
================================================

PURPOSE
-------

Learn the different ways to pass data from Laravel routes and
controllers to Blade views, and understand when to use each method.

Example project: Employee Management System.


1. USING view() WITH with()
---------------------------

This method passes a variable to a Blade view.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function show(int $id)
{
    $employee = Employee::findOrFail($id);

    return view('employees.show')
        ->with('employee', $employee);
}

VIEW FILE:
resources/views/employees/show.blade.php

<h1>Employee Details</h1>

<p>Name: {{ $employee->name }}</p>
<p>Salary: {{ $employee->salary }}</p>

EXPLANATION:

view('employees.show')
    Renders resources/views/employees/show.blade.php.

with('employee', $employee)
    Makes the $employee variable available inside the view.

WHEN TO USE:
When you want to explicitly attach data to a view.


2. USING AN ASSOCIATIVE ARRAY
-----------------------------

This method passes one or more variables to a view.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function show(int $id)
{
    $employee = Employee::findOrFail($id);

    $companyName = 'ABC Technologies';

    return view('employees.show', [
        'employee' => $employee,
        'companyName' => $companyName,
    ]);
}

VIEW:

<h1>{{ $companyName }}</h1>

<p>Name: {{ $employee->name }}</p>
<p>Salary: {{ $employee->salary }}</p>

EXPLANATION:

The array keys become variable names in the view.

'employee' => $employee
    Makes $employee available.

'companyName' => $companyName
    Makes $companyName available.

WHEN TO USE:
This is a good default when passing multiple variables
from a controller to a Blade view.


3. USING compact()
------------------

compact() creates an associative array from existing PHP variables.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function show(int $id)
{
    $employee = Employee::findOrFail($id);

    $companyName = 'ABC Technologies';

    return view(
        'employees.show',
        compact('employee', 'companyName')
    );
}

VIEW:

<h1>{{ $companyName }}</h1>

<p>Name: {{ $employee->name }}</p>
<p>Salary: {{ $employee->salary }}</p>

EXPLANATION:

compact('employee', 'companyName')

Produces an array equivalent to:

[
    'employee' => $employee,
    'companyName' => $companyName,
]

IMPORTANT:
The variables must already exist before you use compact().

WHEN TO USE:
When you already have PHP variables and want to pass them
to a view concisely.


4. DIFFERENCE BETWEEN with(), ARRAY, AND compact()
--------------------------------------------------

METHOD A: with()

return view('employees.show')
    ->with('employee', $employee)
    ->with('companyName', $companyName);

METHOD B: ASSOCIATIVE ARRAY

return view('employees.show', [
    'employee' => $employee,
    'companyName' => $companyName,
]);

METHOD C: compact()

return view(
    'employees.show',
    compact('employee', 'companyName')
);

All three approaches can make the same variables available.

RECOMMENDATION:
Use an associative array as your default.
Use compact() when it makes your code shorter and clearer.
Use with() when you prefer explicitly attaching variables.


5. PASSING A COLLECTION OF RECORDS
----------------------------------

Use this when displaying multiple employees.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function index()
{
    $employees = Employee::all();

    return view('employees.index', [
        'employees' => $employees,
    ]);
}

VIEW:
resources/views/employees/index.blade.php

<h1>Employee List</h1>

@forelse ($employees as $employee)
    <p>
        {{ $employee->name }}
        - {{ $employee->salary }}
    </p>
@empty
    <p>No employees found.</p>
@endforelse

EXPLANATION:

Employee::all()
    Retrieves all employee records as an Eloquent collection.

@forelse
    Loops through the collection.

@empty
    Displays a message if the collection is empty.

WHEN TO USE:
Employee lists, leave application histories, attendance records,
and other pages displaying multiple database records.

IMPORTANT:
For large tables, prefer pagination instead of Employee::all().


6. PASSING ONLY SPECIFIC COLUMNS USING select()
-----------------------------------------------

Use this when the view needs only a few columns.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function create()
{
    $employees = Employee::query()
        ->select('id', 'name')
        ->orderBy('name')
        ->get();

    return view('employees.create', [
        'employees' => $employees,
    ]);
}

VIEW:

<select name="employee_id">
    @foreach ($employees as $employee)
        <option value="{{ $employee->id }}">
            {{ $employee->name }}
        </option>
    @endforeach
</select>

EXPLANATION:

select('id', 'name')
    Requests only the specified columns from the database.

get()
    Executes the query and retrieves the results.

WHEN TO USE:
Dropdowns, search suggestions, lightweight tables, and pages
that do not need every model attribute.


7. USING pluck()
----------------

Use pluck() when you need a list of values rather than complete
model objects.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function index()
{
    $employeeNames = Employee::pluck('name');

    return view('employees.names', [
        'employeeNames' => $employeeNames,
    ]);
}

VIEW:

<h1>Employee Names</h1>

<ul>
    @foreach ($employeeNames as $name)
        <li>{{ $name }}</li>
    @endforeach
</ul>

You can also key the values by another column:

$employeeNames = Employee::pluck('name', 'id');

Conceptual result:

1 => Amit
2 => Rahul
3 => Priya

WHEN TO USE:
Dropdowns, lists of names, status values, and other situations
where complete models are unnecessary.


8. ELOQUENT with() VERSUS VIEW with()
-------------------------------------

There are two different uses of with().

A. ELOQUENT RELATIONSHIP LOADING

$employees = Employee::with('department')->get();

This loads employees together with their department relationship.

B. PASSING DATA TO A VIEW

return view('employees.index')
    ->with('employees', $employees);

This makes the $employees variable available inside the view.

EXAMPLE VIEW:

@foreach ($employees as $employee)
    <p>
        {{ $employee->name }}
        -
        {{ $employee->department?->department_name ?? 'No Department' }}
    </p>
@endforeach

WHEN TO USE ELOQUENT with():
When the view needs related model data and you want to avoid
unnecessary database queries.


9. USING View::share()
----------------------

Use View::share() when the same data needs to be available
across many views.

FILE:
app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php

EXAMPLE:

<?php

namespace App\Providers;

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\View;
use Illuminate\Support\ServiceProvider;

class AppServiceProvider extends ServiceProvider
{
    public function boot(): void
    {
        View::share('companyName', 'ABC Technologies');
    }
}

Now the variable can be used in a Blade view:

<h1>{{ $companyName }}</h1>

WHEN TO USE:
For common values needed throughout many views.

IMPORTANT:
Avoid globally sharing large or sensitive user-specific datasets
without a clear reason.


10. USING VIEW COMPOSERS
------------------------

A view composer prepares data whenever a particular view renders.

Example requirement:
A sidebar should display the total employee count on multiple pages.

CREATE CLASS:

File:
app/View/Composers/EmployeeSidebarComposer.php

<?php

namespace App\View\Composers;

use App\Models\Employee;
use Illuminate\View\View;

class EmployeeSidebarComposer
{
    public function compose(View $view): void
    {
        $view->with(
            'employeeCount',
            Employee::count()
        );
    }
}

REGISTER THE COMPOSER:

File:
app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php

Add these imports:

use App\View\Composers\EmployeeSidebarComposer;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\View;

Register it inside boot():

public function boot(): void
{
    View::composer(
        'partials.sidebar',
        EmployeeSidebarComposer::class
    );
}

If boot() already contains code, keep it and add the registration.

CREATE THE VIEW:

File:
resources/views/partials/sidebar.blade.php

<aside>
    <h2>Employee Management</h2>

    <p>Total employees: {{ $employeeCount }}</p>
</aside>

INCLUDE THE VIEW:

@include('partials.sidebar')

WHEN TO USE:
When a specific reusable view needs data across multiple pages.

DIFFERENCE:
View::share() makes data broadly available.
View::composer() prepares data for particular views.


11. PASSING DATA TO A BLADE COMPONENT
------------------------------------

Components are useful for reusable UI elements.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function show(int $id)
{
    $employee = Employee::findOrFail($id);

    return view('employees.show', [
        'employee' => $employee,
    ]);
}

PARENT VIEW:
resources/views/employees/show.blade.php

<h1>Employee Details</h1>

<x-employee-card :employee="$employee" />

CREATE COMPONENT:

php artisan make:component EmployeeCard

COMPONENT VIEW:
resources/views/components/employee-card.blade.php

<div class="employee-card">
    <h2>{{ $employee->name }}</h2>

    <p>Salary: {{ $employee->salary }}</p>
</div>

EXPLANATION:

:employee="$employee"
    Passes the PHP variable into the component.

WHEN TO USE:
Employee cards, form fields, buttons, status badges, and reusable
UI elements.

A component does not automatically retrieve database records.
The required data must be provided to it.


12. PASSING PAGINATED DATA
--------------------------

Use pagination when a table contains many records.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;

public function index()
{
    $employees = Employee::orderBy('id')
        ->paginate(10);

    return view('employees.index', [
        'employees' => $employees,
    ]);
}

VIEW:

<h1>Employees</h1>

@foreach ($employees as $employee)
    <p>{{ $employee->name }}</p>
@endforeach

{{ $employees->links() }}

EXPLANATION:

paginate(10)
    Retrieves a page containing up to 10 records.

links()
    Renders pagination controls.

WHEN TO USE:
Large employee lists, leave histories, attendance records,
and administrative reports.


13. PASSING VALIDATION ERRORS AND OLD INPUT
-------------------------------------------

When form validation fails during a normal web request,
Laravel redirects back and makes validation errors available
to the view.

CONTROLLER:

use Illuminate\Http\Request;

public function store(Request $request)
{
    $validated = $request->validate([
        'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'salary' => ['required', 'numeric', 'min:0'],
    ]);

    // Save the validated data here.
}

VIEW:

<form method="POST" action="{{ route('employees.store') }}">
    @csrf

    <label for="name">Name</label>

    <input
        type="text"
        id="name"
        name="name"
        value="{{ old('name') }}"
    >

    @error('name')
        <p>{{ $message }}</p>
    @enderror

    <label for="salary">Salary</label>

    <input
        type="number"
        id="salary"
        name="salary"
        value="{{ old('salary') }}"
    >

    @error('salary')
        <p>{{ $message }}</p>
    @enderror

    <button type="submit">Save Employee</button>
</form>

EXPLANATION:

old('name')
    Retrieves previously submitted input when available.

@error('name')
    Displays the validation error for the name field.

WHEN TO USE:
Forms that need to preserve submitted values and display
validation errors.


14. REDIRECTING WITH A SUCCESS MESSAGE
--------------------------------------

After saving data, you may redirect the user to another page
and display a success message.

CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

public function store(Request $request)
{
    $validated = $request->validate([
        'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'salary' => ['required', 'numeric', 'min:0'],
    ]);

    Employee::create($validated);

    return redirect()
        ->route('employees.index')
        ->with('success', 'Employee created successfully.');
}

DESTINATION VIEW:

@if (session('success'))
    <div class="alert alert-success">
        {{ session('success') }}
    </div>
@endif

EXPLANATION:

redirect()->route('employees.index')
    Redirects the browser to the named route.

with('success', '...')
    Stores a flash message in the session for the next request.

WHEN TO USE:
After creating, updating, approving, or deleting a record.

IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE:

view()->with()
    Passes data to the view being rendered.

redirect()->with()
    Flashes data to the session for a subsequent request.


15. RETURNING A VIEW DIRECTLY FROM A ROUTE
------------------------------------------

For a simple page, you do not always need a controller.

FILE:
routes/web.php

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;

Route::view('/about', 'about');

This renders:

resources/views/about.blade.php

You can also pass data:

Route::view('/about', 'about', [
    'companyName' => 'ABC Technologies',
]);

VIEW:

<h1>About {{ $companyName }}</h1>

WHEN TO USE:
Simple static pages that do not need controller logic.

For pages involving database queries, validation, or business
rules, a controller is generally more suitable.


16. RETURNING A VIEW VERSUS JSON
--------------------------------

A. RETURN A BLADE VIEW

return view('employees.show', [
    'employee' => $employee,
]);

Purpose:
Render HTML for a web page.

B. RETURN JSON

return response()->json([
    'id' => $employee->id,
    'name' => $employee->name,
    'salary' => $employee->salary,
]);

Purpose:
Return structured JSON data to a client.

COMPARISON:

view()
    Returns rendered HTML.
    Used for server-rendered web pages.
    Uses Blade templates.

response()->json()
    Returns JSON.
    Used for APIs and clients expecting JSON.
    Does not render a Blade template.

For a project with both web and mobile interfaces, keep the
web and API layers separate while reusing business logic
through service classes where appropriate.


17. HOW DATA REACHES THE BLADE VIEW
-----------------------------------

CONTROLLER:

public function show(int $id)
{
    $employee = Employee::findOrFail($id);

    $companyName = 'ABC Technologies';

    return view('employees.show', [
        'employee' => $employee,
        'companyName' => $companyName,
    ]);
}

VIEW:

<h1>{{ $companyName }}</h1>

<p>{{ $employee->name }}</p>

PROCESS:

1. Laravel receives the request.
2. The route invokes the controller.
3. The controller retrieves the employee.
4. The controller prepares the required variables.
5. The controller returns a view response with those variables.
6. Laravel renders the Blade template.
7. Blade evaluates the variables and generates HTML.
8. Laravel returns the HTML response to the browser.


18. QUICK REFERENCE TABLE
-------------------------

REQUIREMENT
    RECOMMENDED METHOD

Send one variable to a view
    view()->with()

Send multiple variables
    Associative array

Send existing PHP variables concisely
    compact()

Display one employee
    Retrieve a model and pass it to the view

Display multiple employees
    Retrieve a collection and pass it to the view

Retrieve selected columns
    select() followed by get()

Retrieve a single column
    value()

Retrieve a collection of column values
    pluck()

Load related models
    Eloquent with()

Share data across views
    View::share()

Prepare data for specific views
    View composers

Pass data into a reusable UI element
    Blade component attributes

Display a large collection
    paginate()

Preserve previous form input
    old()

Display validation errors
    @error or $errors

Redirect with a success message
    redirect()->with()

Display a simple static page
    Route::view()

Return data to a mobile app
    response()->json()


19. FINAL RECOMMENDATION
------------------------

For most Laravel CRUD pages, use this pattern.

CONTROLLER:

public function index()
{
    $employees = Employee::orderBy('name')->paginate(10);

    return view('employees.index', [
        'employees' => $employees,
    ]);
}

BLADE VIEW:

<h1>Employees</h1>

@foreach ($employees as $employee)
    <p>{{ $employee->name }}</p>
@endforeach

{{ $employees->links() }}

REMEMBER:

1. Retrieve data in the controller or an appropriate service.
2. Pass the required data to the view.
3. Display it in Blade.
4. Use pagination for large datasets.
5. Use view composers for data repeatedly required by specific
   views.
6. Use Blade components for reusable UI elements.
7. Use services for business logic that needs to be reused.
8. Use JSON responses for endpoints designed to serve APIs.

THE MOST IMPORTANT PATTERN:

DATABASE
    |
    v
CONTROLLER
    |
    v
VIEW RESPONSE WITH DATA
    |
    v
BLADE TEMPLATE
    |
    v
HTML RESPONSE TO THE BROWSER
